{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Settling the question raised by 46a2-9c1a-d855-4706 (does the 131x-repeat measurement contradict this ticket's breadth premise?): BOTH exhaustion modes are real — they are siblings, not one bug. Instrumented evidence from the c827 investigation: of four traced exhaustions of this gate, three LOOPED (one repeated an identical search_files call 144x; 46a2's case repeated one 131x; bf31's was a 4-call cycle repeated 31-37x) and ONE was genuine breadth (258 calls, 238 distinct, ratio 0.922, minimum 24-window distinct-ratio 0.833 — no loop by any metric). This ticket's premise holds for that run class; re-scope it to the breadth mode. The loop mode is being fixed by epic 9b71-55b3-1bd1-4a97 (windowed distinct-ratio detector + route-to-recovery actuator). Per the operator's stated intent, a breadth exhaustion under a budget that bounds FAILURES is correct behaviour — this ticket's remaining scope is making evidence-gathering more directed, not raising ceilings."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785954775605464001,"uuid":"938148a7-0c21-4eb4-a8d4-0d1485ca3310"}